<Section id="arraysList">

## Introduction

In the previous units, we learned arrays in Java. These arrays are fixed in size, which makes them difficult to use when we need to add/remove elements once the array is defined.

This can be achieved with **ArrayLists**. The size of ArrayLists is dynamic, i.e, the size of the ArrayList can be changed.

In this unit, we'll learn **ArrayLists** and perform some basic operations on it.

## 1. ArrayList

The `ArrayList` class from the package `java.util` is used to implement the functionality of resizable arrays.

The ArrayLists adjusts its size automatically when an element is added or removed. Hence, it is also known as a Dynamic Array.

An ArrayList can only store objects, for example, `String`. We cannot store primitive data types directly.

> We will learn more about storing primitive data types later in this unit.

#### Syntax

```java
ArrayList<Type> arrList = new ArrayList<>();
```

Here,

- `ArrayList` is the class name
- `arrList` is the name of the variable
- `Type` is the data type of the `arrList`

Let's create an ArrayList named `players` of type `String`

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();
    }
}
```

</Section>

<Section id ="addingElementsToArrayList">

## 2. Adding Elements to ArrayList

The `add()` method is used to add a single element to the ArrayList.

#### Syntax

```java
arrList.add(index, element);
```

Here,

- `arrList`: an object of `ArrayList` class
- `index` (optional): at which the element is to be inserted
- `element`: the element to be inserted

The `add()` method works similarly to the `insert` method in Python.

If the `index` is not passed, the `element` is appended to the end of the ArrayList.

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();

        players.add(0, "Bryant");
        players.add("Wade"); // adding element without providing index
    }
}
```

</Section>

<Section id="accessingArrayListElements">

## 3. Accessing ArrayList Elements

In Arrays, we can directly access and modify elements using the bracket `[]` syntax, but in ArrayLists, we have separate methods for accessing and modifying the ArrayList.

The `get()` method is used to access an element from an ArrayList.

#### Syntax

```java
arrList.get(index);
```

Here, the `index` specifies the position in the ArrayList from which we need to get the element.

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();

        players.add(0, "Bryant");
        players.add("Wade");

        System.out.println(players.get(1));
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

Wade
```

Here, we have used the `get()` method with the argument as `1`. Hence, it returned the element at index `1`.

