>
> ```
> Before Modification: [Bryant, Wade]
> After Modification: [Bryant, Garnett]
> ```
>
> </details>

</Section>
<Section id = "addingPrimitiveDataTypesToArrayLists">

## 6. Adding Primitive Data Types to ArrayLists

ArrayList can only store objects. To use primitive data types, we have to convert them to objects.

In Java, **Wrapper Classes** are can be used to convert primitive types (int, char, float, etc) into corresponding objects.

Below are the Wrapper Classes for the corresponding primitive types.

| Primitive Type | Wrapper Class |
| :------------: | :-----------: |
|      byte      |     Byte      |
|    boolean     |    Boolean    |
|      char      |   Character   |
|     double     |    Double     |
|     float      |     Float     |
|      int       |    Integer    |
|      long      |     Long      |
|     short      |     Short     |

Let's look at an example,

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> points = new ArrayList<>();

        points.add(56);
        points.add(75);
        points.add(84);

        System.out.println(points);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

[56, 75, 84]
```

In the above code, we have used the `Integer` wrapper class to specify the type while creating an ArrayList.

</Section>

<Section id = "autoboxingAndunboxing">

## 7. Autoboxing and Unboxing

Autoboxing and unboxing are the processes in which the Java compiler automatically converts primitive types into their corresponding wrapper class objects and vice-versa.

### 7.1 Autoboxing

The conversion of primitive types into their corresponding wrapper class objects is called **Autoboxing**.

For example: `int` --> `Integer`, `double` --> `Double`, etc

#### Code

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 23;
        Integer numObj = num; // autoboxing

        System.out.println(numObj);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

23
```

In the above code, at the statement `Integer numObj = num` the Java compiler automatically converts the number `23` from `int` to `Integer` object and assigns it to the `numObj`.

### 7.2 Unboxing

The conversion of wrapper class objects into their corresponding primitive types is called **Unboxing**.

For example: `Integer` --> `int`, `Double` --> `double`, etc

#### Code

```java
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer numObj = 23;
        int num = numObj; // unboxing

        System.out.println(num);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

23
```

In the above code, at the statement `int num = numObj` the Java compiler automatically converts the number `23` from `Integer` object to `int` and assigns it to the `num`.

</Section>

<Section id = "removingArrayListElements" >

## 8. Removing ArrayList Elements

The `remove()` method works similar to the `pop()` method in Python.

It can accept either an `int` value or an object as an argument.

### 8.1 Removing Elements using Index

When an integer is passed as an argument, the `remove()` method removes the element at the specified position, i.e index, in the ArrayList.

#### Syntax

```java
arrList.remove(index);
```

Here, `index` is an integer value.

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();

        players.add("Bryant");
        players.add("Wade");
        players.add("Garnett");
        players.add("Rose");

        System.out.println("Before Removing: " + players);

        players.remove(1); // removing the element at index 1

        System.out.println("After Removing: " + players);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Before Removing: [Bryant, Wade, Garnett, Rose]
After Removing: [Bryant, Garnett, Rose]
```

In the above code, index `1` is passed to the `remove()` method. This removes the element at index `1`, i.e, `Wade` from the ArrayList.

### 8.2 Removing Elements using an Element

when an object is passed as an argument, the `remove()` method removes the first occurrence of the specified element from the ArrayList if it is present. If the ArrayList does not contain the element, the ArrayList remains unchanged.

#### Syntax

```java
arrayList.remove(obj);
```

Here, `obj` is an object. For example, `String` values or the wrapper class objects of primitives.

_Example 1:_

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();

        players.add("Bryant");
        players.add("Wade");
        players.add("Garnett");
        players.add("Rose");

        System.out.println("Before Removing: " + players);

        players.remove("Garnett"); // removing the first occurrence of the element "Garnett"

        System.out.println("After Removing: " + players);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Before Removing: [Bryant, Wade, Garnett, Rose]
After Removing: [Bryant, Wade, Rose]
```

In the above code, the element `Garnett` is passed to the `remove()` method. This removes the element `Garnett` from the ArrayList.

_Example 2:_

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

        numbers.add(8);  // autoboxing
        numbers.add(3);  // autoboxing
        numbers.add(5);  // autoboxing
        numbers.add(1);  // autoboxing

        System.out.println("Before Removing: " + numbers);

        numbers.remove((Integer)5); // removing the first occurrence of the element 5

        System.out.println("After Removing: " + numbers);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Before Removing: [8, 3, 5, 1]
After Removing: [8, 3, 1]
```

In the above code, we have used the wrapper class `Integer` to explicitly convert the `int` value 5 to its corresponding object. Here, autoboxing does not take place because the `remove()` method also accepts index (`int` type) as argument and removes the element at the specified index.

### 8.3 clear()

The `clear()` method works similar to that in Python. It completely removes all of the elements from the ArrayList.

#### Syntax

```java
arrList.clear();
```

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();

        players.add("Bryant");
        players.add("Wade");
        players.add("Garnett");
        players.add("Rose");

        System.out.println(players) ;

        players.clear(); // removing all the elements from the Arraylist

        System.out.println(players) ;
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
[Bryant, Wade, Garnett, Rose]
[]
```

</Section>

<Section id="sizeOfAnArrayList">

## 9. Size of an ArrayList

The `size()` method is used to find the size of an ArrayList.

#### Syntax

```java
arrList.size();
```

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> points = new ArrayList<>();

        points.add(56); // autoboxing
        points.add(75); // autoboxing
        points.add(84); // autoboxing

        System.out.println(points);
        System.out.println(points.size());
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
[56, 75, 84]
3
```

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Array**</summary>
> <br />
>
> For Arrays, we use the `length` attribute to find the length.
>
> #### Code
>
> ```java
> class Main {
>      public static void main(String[] args) {
>          int[] points = {56, 75, 84};
>          int lengthOfArr = points.length;
>
>          System.out.println(lengthOfArr);
>    }
> }
>
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
>
> 3
> ```
>
> </details>

</Section>

<Section id="iterationOverAnArrayList">

## 10. Iterating over an ArrayList

Similar to iterating over an array, we can use the loops to iterate over an ArrayList.

Let's see iterating over an ArrayList using the `for-each` loop.

#### Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> players = new ArrayList<>();

        players.add(0, "Bryant");
        players.add("Wade");
        players.add("Garnett");
        players.add("Rose");

        for (String name : players) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Bryant
Wade
Garnett
Rose
```

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Array**</summary>
>
> #### Code
>
> ```java
> class Main {
>     public static void main(String[] args) {
>         String[] players = {"Bryant", "Wade", "Garnett", "Rose"};
>
>         for(String name: players) {
>             System.out.println(name);
>        }
>    }
> }
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> Bryant
> Wade
> Garnett
> Rose
> ```
>
> </details>

</Section>

<Section>

### Summary

- The size of ArrayLists is dynamic, i.e, the size of the ArrayList can be changed.

- In Java, ArrayList can only store objects. To store primitive data types, they need to be converted to their corresponding objects.

- Wrapper classes can be used to convert primitive data types into objects.
- **Autoboxing**: The conversion of primitive types into their corresponding wrapper class objects is called autoboxing.
- **Unboxing**: The conversion of wrapper class objects into their corresponding primitive types is called unboxing.

- ArrayList methods:

  - `add()`: used to add a single element to an ArrayList.

  - `get()`: used to access an element from an ArrayList.

  - `set()`: used to modify an element of an ArrayList.

  - `remove(index)`: removes the element at a specified position, i.e index, in the ArrayList.

  - `remove(object)`: removes the first occurrence of the specified element from the ArrayList, if it is present. If the ArrayList does not contain the element, the ArrayList remains unchanged.

  - `size()`: used to find the size of an ArrayList.

- The `for-each` loop can be used to iterate over ArrayLists.

</Section>

