## 4. Superset and Subset

The `containsAll()` method can be used to check if a given set is a superset or subset of the given set.

### 4.1 Superset

A superset of any given set is defined as the set which contains all the elements present in the given set.

#### Syntax

```java
hset1.containsAll(hset2);
```

Here, `containsAll()` checks if all the elements in `hset2` are present in `hset1`, i.e, it checks if `hset1` is a superset of `hset2`

_Example 1:_

#### Code

```java
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashSet<Integer> hset1 = new HashSet<>();
        HashSet<Integer> hset2 = new HashSet<>();

        hset1.add(3);
        hset1.add(32);
        hset1.add(8);
        hset1.add(5);


        hset2.add(8);
        hset2.add(3);

        System.out.println("hset1: " + hset1);
        System.out.println("hset2: " + hset2);

        boolean superset = hset1.containsAll(hset2); // checks if hset1 is superset of hset2

        System.out.println("\nhset1 is superset of hset2:  " + superset);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
hset1: [32, 3, 5, 8]
hset2: [3, 8]

hset1 is superset of hset2:  true
```

