<Section id="references">

## Introduction

In the previous unit, we learned **HashMaps** and performed some basic operations like adding elements, modifying values, etc.

In this unit, we'll learn some more operations like copying, finding the size of a HashMap, etc.

## 1. HashMap References

Let's consider that `hmap1` and `hmap2` are two HashMap variables.

When the HashMap `hmap2` is assigned with an existing HashMap `hmap1`, both the HashMap variables `hmap1` and `hmap2` will refer to the same object.

#### Code

```java
import java.util.HashMap;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashMap<String, Integer> hmap1 = new HashMap<>();
        HashMap<String, Integer> hmap2 = new HashMap<>();

        hmap1.put("Robert", 145);
        hmap1.put("James", 121);
        hmap1.put("Antony", 136);
        hmap1.put("John", 78);

        hmap2 = hmap1;  // both the variables point to the same referrence

        System.out.println("Before Modifying: " + hmap2); // printing hmap2

        hmap1.replace("John", 100); // modifying a value in hmap1

        System.out.println("After Modifying: " + hmap2); // printing hmap2
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Before Modifying: {James=121, Robert=145, John=78, Antony=136}
After Modifying: {James=121, Robert=145, John=100, Antony=136}
```

In the above code, the `hmap2` points to the HashMap referenced by `hmap1`. Hence, if we modify the HashMap using any of the HashMaps (`hmap1` or `hmap2`), then it will be reflected in the other HashMap too.

</Section>

<Section id="copy">

## 2. Copying HashMaps

The `putAll()` method can be used to copy all the elements from a HashMap to another HashMap.

#### Syntax

```java
hmap2.putAll(hmap1);
```

Here,

- `hmap1` is the existing HashMap
- `hmap2` is the new HashMap to which `hmap1` is to be copied

_Example 1:_

#### Code

```java
import java.util.HashMap;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashMap<String, Integer> hmap1 = new HashMap<>();
        HashMap<String, Integer> hmap2 = new HashMap<>();

        hmap1.put("Robert", 145);
        hmap1.put("James", 121);
        hmap1.put("Antony", 136);
        hmap1.put("John", 78);

        hmap2.putAll(hmap1);  // copying all elements from hmap1 to hmap2

        hmap1.replace("John", 100); // modifying a value in hmap1


        System.out.println("hmap1: " + hmap1);
        System.out.println("hmap2: " + hmap2);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
hmap1: {James=121, Robert=145, John=100, Antony=136}
hmap2: {James=121, Robert=145, John=78, Antony=136}
```

In the above code, we can see that all the elements from `hmap1` are copied to `hmap2`.

The values modified in `hmap1` will not affect the values of `hmap2` or vice versa, except for non-primitive values like Arrays, ArrayLists, etc.

_Example 2:_

#### Code

```java
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> arrList = new ArrayList<>();
        HashMap<String, ArrayList> hmap1 = new HashMap<>();
        HashMap<String, ArrayList> hmap2 = new HashMap<>();

        arrList.add(2);
        arrList.add(3);

        hmap1.put("scores", arrList);
        hmap2.putAll(hmap1);  // copying all elements from hmap1 to hmap2
        arrList.set(1, 10);

        System.out.println("hmap1: " + hmap1);
        System.out.println("hmap2: " + hmap2);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
hmap1: {scores=[2, 10]}
hmap2: {scores=[2, 10]}
```

In the above code, we can see that modifying the `arrList` has affected the values in both `hmap1` and `hmap2`.

The non-primitive values share the reference instead of creating a new value. Thus, when a non-primitive mutates, all values that share its reference will be affected.

</Section>

