## 2. Method Overloading

If a class has multiple methods having same name but different in parameters, it is known as Method Overloading.

There are two ways to overload the method in java

- By changing the data type of Parameters
- By changing the number of Parameters

### 2.1 Method Overloading by changing the data type of Parameters

We can overload methods by changing the data type of parameters for every method to have multiple methods with the same method name.

#### Code

```java
class Main {
    static void addition(int a, int b) {
        // a and b  are called method parameters

        int sum = a + b;
        System.out.println("The sum of 2 int numbers " + a + " and " + b + " is " + sum);
    }

    static void addition(double a, double b) {
        // a and b are called method parameters

        double sum = a + b;
        System.out.println("The sum of 2 float numbers " + a + " and " + b + " is " + sum);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int value1 = 20;
        int value2 = 34;
        double value3 = 45.6;
        double value4 = 32.3;

        addition(value1, value2);
        addition(value3, value4);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
The sum of 2 int numbers 20 and 34 is 54
The sum of 2 float numbers 45.6 and 32.3 is 77.9
```

There are two methods with the same name `addition()`, and they differ in the type of parameters that are declared during their method declaration.

One of the most popular examples of this way of method overloading is the `println()` method in `System.out.println()` whose job is to print data on the console. This method is overloaded to accept all kinds of data types in Java.

We have multiple methods declared with the name `println()` which takes String, int, float, double or char as an argument. All of those methods are collectively referred to as overloaded methods in Java.

### 2.2. Method Overloading by changing the number of Parameters

We can overload methods by changing the number of parameters for each method to have multiple methods with the same method name.

#### Code

```java
class Main {
    static void addition(int a, int b) {
        // a and b are called method parameters

        int sum = a + b;
        System.out.println("The sum of 2 numbers " + a + " and " + b + " is " + sum);
    }

    static void addition(int a, int b, int c) {
        // a, b and c are called method parameters

        int sum = a + b + c;
        System.out.println("The sum of 3 numbers " + a + ", " + b + " and " + c + " is " + sum);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int value1 = 20;
        int value2 = 34;
        int value3 = 45;

        addition(value1, value2);
        addition(value1, value2, value3);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
The sum of 2 numbers 20 and 34 is 54
The sum of 3 numbers 20, 34 and 45 is 99
```

There are two methods with the same name `addition()`, and they differ in the number of parameters that are declared during their method declaration.

### 2.3 Possible Mistake

If there are different `return` types and the same method name with the same number of parameters and the same type of parameters, the method will not be overloaded

#### Incorrect Code

```java|UGLY|ITALIC
class Main {
    static int addition(int a, int b) {
        int sum = a + b;

        System.out.println("The sum of 2 numbers " + a + " and " + b + " is " + sum);
    }

    static void addition(int a, int b) {
        int sum = a + b;

        System.out.println("The sum of 3 numbers " + a + ", " + b + " and " + c + " is " + sum);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int value1 = 20;
        int value2 = 34;

        int result = addition(value1, value2);

        addition(value1, value2);

        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
file.java:10: error: method addition(int,int) is already defined in class Main
    static void addition(int a,int b) {
                ^
1 error
```

</Section>

<Section id ="mut">

## 3. Passing Mutable Objects

Similar to Python, when mutable objects are passed as method arguments, the changes done to the object inside the method will affect the original object.

Arrays can be passed as arguments to methods just like primitive data types. To pass an array as an argument to a method, we just have to specify the name of the array variable.

#### Code

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Main {
    static void twice(int[] arr2 ) {
        int n = arr2.length;

        for(int i = 0; i < n ; i++){
            arr2[i] = 2 * arr2[i];
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr1 = {36,43,10,112,66,18};

        System.out.println("Before modifying: " + Arrays.toString(arr1));

        twice(arr1);

        System.out.println("After modifying: " +  Arrays.toString(arr1));
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Before modifying: [36, 43, 10, 112, 66, 18]
After modifying: [72, 86, 20, 224, 132, 36]
```

In the above code,

In `twice()` method declaration, its parameter `int[] arr2`, the presence of data type followed by a pair of square brackets and array name indicates that this method needs an array to be passed at the calling time.

In the statement `twice(arr1)` reference of the array that is pointed by `arr1` is passed.

Inside the `twice()` method, `arr2` will store the array reference passed during the calling time.

Both `arr1` and `arr2` have the same reference. If we modify array elements using `arr2` then it will affect the array.

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> def change_list(list_b):
>     n = len(list_b)
>     for i in range(n):
>         list_b[i] = 2 * list_b[i]
>
> list_a = [36, 43, 10, 112, 66, 18]
> print("Before modifying: " + str(list_a))
> change_list(list_a)
> print("After modifying: " + str(list_a))
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> Before modifying: [36, 43, 10, 112, 66, 18]
> After modifying: [72, 86, 20, 224, 132, 36]
> ```
>
> </details>

 </Section>

 <Section id ="immu">

## 4. Passing Immutable Objects

Similar to Python, immutable objects are passed as method arguments, the changes done to the object inside the method will not affect the original object.

#### Code

```java
class Main {
    static void fullName(String str2) {
        str2 = "William Smith";

        System.out.println("Inside fullName() method: " + str2);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "William";

        fullName(str1);

        System.out.println("Inside main() method: " + str1);
     }
}
```

#### Output

```
Inside fullName() method: William Smith
Inside main() method: William
```

In the above code, `fullName()` method is accepting one `String` argument.

When calling the `fullName()` method, `str1` value will be passed and gets stored in the `str2` variable. Since strings in Java are immutable, any changes done on `str2` inside the `fullName()` method will not affect `str1`.

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> def full_name(str_2):
>     str_2 = "William Smith"
>     print("Inside function: " + str_2)
>
> str1 = "William"
> full_name(str1)
> print("Outside function: " + str1)
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> Inside function: William Smith
> Outside function: William
> ```
>
> </details>

 </Section>

<Section>

### Summary

- In Java, only through positional arguments, we can pass the arguments to the methods.

- Method overloading is achieved by either:

  - changing the number of arguments.
  - changing the data type of arguments.

- Method overloading is not possible on the basis of the return type.

- When mutable objects are passed as method arguments, the changes done to the object inside the method will affect the original object.

- When immutable objects are passed as method arguments, the changes done to the object inside the method will not affect the original object.

</Section>

