<Section id="superKeyword">

## 3. The `super` Keyword

The `super` is the keyword in Java, used to access the superclass members like attributes, methods, and also the constructors inside the subclass.

**Uses of `super` keyword**

- To access the attributes of the superclass when both superclass and subclass have attributes with the same name.
- To call the overridden methods of the superclass inside the subclass.
- To invoke the superclass constructor having parameters explicitly from the subclass constructor.

### 3.1 Accessing Attributes using `super` Keyword

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    String type = "animal";
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    String type = "mammal";

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Horse is a " + type);
        System.out.println("Horse is an " + super.type);  // accessing the superclass attribute
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse();
        horse.display();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Horse is a mammal
Horse is an animal
```

In the above code, we have accessed the superclass attribute `type` inside the subclass using the `super` keyword.

### 3.2 Invoking Methods using `super` Keyword

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    void eat() {  // overriding the eat() method of Animal class
        System.out.println("Horse eats grass");
        super.eat();  // invoking the overridden method of the Animal class
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse();
        horse.eat();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Horse eats grass
Eating
```

In the above code, we have invoked the overridden method `eat()` of the `Animal` class inside the subclass using the `super` keyword.

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
> <br />
> In Python, `super()` is a built-in function that only allows us to access methods of the superclass.
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> class Animal:
>     def __init__(self):
>         self.type = "animal"
>
>     def eat(self):
>         print("Eating")
>
> class Horse(Animal):
>     def eat(self):  # overriding the eat() method of Animal class
>         print("Horse eats grass")
>         super().eat()  # invoking the overridden method of the Animal class
>
> horse = Horse()
> horse.eat()
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> Horse eats grass
> Eating
> ```
>
> </details>

### 3.3 Invoking Constructors using `super()`

The constructors which do not accept arguments are also called _non-parameterized constructors_.
The constructors which accept arguments are also called _parameterized constructors_.

**3.3.1 Invoking non-parameterized superclass constructor**

The Java compiler implicitly calls the non-parameterized superclass constructor if we do not call the superclass constructor explicitly.

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    String name;

    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Superclass constructor called");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    String breed;

    Horse(String breed) {
        this.breed = breed;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse("Shire");
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

Superclass constructor called
```

In the above code, we can see that the superclass constructor has been called even though we did not invoke it explicitly.

We can invoke the superclass constructor explicitly as `super()`

When the superclass constructor is invoked explicitly, the `super()` must be the first statement inside the subclass constructor.

**3.3.2 Invoking parameterized superclass constructor**

When the superclass consists of a parameterized constructor, it needs to be called explicitly using the `super()` and pass the required arguments

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    String name;

    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    String breed;

    Horse(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);  // invoking the superclass constructor with an argument
        this.breed = breed;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse("Alex", "Shire");

        System.out.println(horse.name);
        System.out.println(horse.breed);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Alex
Shire
```

In the above code, we have invoked the superclass constructor which accepts a single argument using the `super()` and passed the required argument.

> <details>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
> <br />
> In Python, the `__init__` method can be overriden like other methods. We can invoke the superclass `__init__` method using the `super()` function.
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> class Animal:
>     def __init__(self, name):
>         self.name = name
>
> class Horse(Animal):
>     def __init__(self, name, breed):
>         super().__init__(name)
>         self.breed = breed
>
> horse = Horse("Alex", "Shire")
> print(horse.name)
> print(horse.breed)
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> Alex
> Shire
> ```
>
> </details>

**What if we do not call the superclass constructors which accept arguments?**

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    String name;

    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    void displayName() {
        System.out.println("My name is " + name);
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    String breed;

    Horse(String name, String breed) {
        this.breed = breed;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse("Alex", "Shire");
        horse.displayName();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
file.java:16: error: constructor Animal in class Animal cannot be applied to given types;
    Horse(String name, String breed) {
                                     ^
  required: String
```

In the above code, the superclass `Animal` consists of a parameterized constructor. As we did not invoke the parameterized constructor explicitly, the error has been thrown.

**3.3.3 Superclass with overloaded constructors**

If a superclass consists of overloaded constructors, only one constructor can be invoked.

let's create a superclass with two constructors. One non-parameterized constructor and a parameterized constructor.

- If we do not invoke any of the superclass constructors from the subclass, the Java compiler implicitly invokes the non-parameterized constructor.
- If we invoke any of the superclass constructors from the subclass, then the other constructor will not be invoked.

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    String name;

    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Non-parameterized constructor");
    }

    Animal(String name) {
        System.out.println("Parameterized constructor");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {

    Horse(String name) {
        super(name);  // invoking parameterized superclass constructor
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse("Alex");
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

Parameterized constructor
```

In the above code, the superclass `Animal` consists of two overloaded constructors. We have invoked the parameterized constructor inside the subclass constructor and can see that the non-parameterized constructor is not invoked.

</Section>

<Section id="typesOfInheritance">

## 4. Types of Inheritance

Java majorly supports three types of inheritances with classes,

1. Single Inheritance
2. Multilevel Inheritance
3. Hierarchical Inheritance

### 4.1 Single Inheritance

Single inheritance involves extending a single superclass from a single subclass.

In Java, a subclass can have only one superclass.

<img src="https://s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/java/java-rm-single-inheritance.png" alt="single inheritance"/>

The examples we have seen so far are all examples of single inheritances.

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    String name;

    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    String breed;

    Horse(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);
        this.breed = breed;
    }

}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse("Alex", "Shire");
        System.out.println(horse.name);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

Alex
```

In the above code, there is only a single superclass `Animal` and only a single subclass `Horse` and the `Horse` extends `Animal`. Hence, it is called Single Inheritance.

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> class Animal:
>     def __init__(self, name):
>         self.name = name
>
> class Horse(Animal):
>     def __init__(self, name, breed):
>         super().__init__(name)
>         self.breed = breed
>
> horse = Horse("Alex", "Shire")
> print(horse.name)
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
>
> Alex
> ```
>
> </details>

### 4.2 Multilevel Inheritance

In multilevel inheritance, a subclass extends from a superclass and then the same subclass acts as a superclass for another class.

<img src="https://s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/java/java-rm-multilevel-inheritance.png" alt="multilevel inheritance"/>

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
       System.out.println("I am eating");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    void run() {
       System.out.println("I am running");
    }
}

class ArabianHorse extends Horse {
    void displaySkill() {
       System.out.println("I am known for my strength and stamina");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
       ArabianHorse arabianHorse = new ArabianHorse();
       arabianHorse.eat();
       arabianHorse.run();
       arabianHorse.displaySkill();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
I am eating
I am running
I am known for my strength and stamina
```

In the above code, the `Horse` class extends `Animal` and the `ArabianHorse` class extends `Horse`. The `Horse` class acts as a subclass to `Animal` and a superclass to `ArabianHorse`. Hence, it is called Multilevel Inheritance.

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> class Animal:
>      def eat(self):
>          print("I am eating")
>
> class Horse(Animal):
>      def run(self):
>          print("I am running")
>
> class ArabianHorse(Horse):
>      def display_skill(self):
>          print("I am known for my strength and stamina")
>
> arabian_horse = ArabianHorse()
> arabian_horse.eat()
> arabian_horse.run()
> arabian_horse.display_skill()
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> I am eating
> I am running
> I am known for my strength and stamina
> ```
>
> </details>

### 4.3 Hierarchical Inheritance

In hierarchical inheritance, multiple subclasses extend from a single superclass.

<img src="https://s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/java/java-rm-hierarchical-inheritance.png" alt="hierarchical inheritance"/>

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating ...");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running ...");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    public void bark() {
        System.out.println("Bow bow ...");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Horse horse = new Horse();
        Dog dog = new Dog();

        horse.eat();
        horse.run();
        dog.eat();
        dog.bark();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Eating ...
Running ...
Eating ...
Bow bow ...
```

In the above code, the `Horse` class extends `Animal` and the `Dog` class also extends `Animal`. Here, both `Horse` and `Dog` are the subclasses of a single superclass `Animal`. Hence, it is called Hierarchical Inheritance

> <details open>
> <summary>**Comparison with Python**</summary>
>
> #### Code
>
> ```python
> class Animal:
>     def eat(self):
>         print("Eating ...")
>
> class Horse(Animal):
>     def run(self):
>         print("Running ...")
>
> class Dog(Animal):
>     def bark(self):
>         print("Bow bow ...")
>
> horse = Horse()
> dog = Dog()
>
> horse.eat()
> horse.run()
> dog.eat()
> dog.bark()
> ```
>
> #### Output
>
> ```
> Eating ...
> Running ...
> Eating ...
> Bow bow ...
> ```
>
> </details>

</Section>

<Section id="upcasting">

## 5. Upcasting

In Java, a superclass reference variable can be used to refer to its subclass object i.e., we can specify a superclass as a type while creating an object of its subclass. This process of typecasting a subclass object to a superclass variable is called Upcasting.

In this case, we can access all the members of the superclass but can only access a few members like overriding methods of the subclass.

#### Syntax

```java
Superclass variableName = new Subclass();
```

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    void eat() {  // overrides the eat() method of Animal class
        System.out.println("Horse is eating");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Horse();
    }
}
```

In the above code, we specified the type of `animal` variable as `Animal` and assigned it an object of `Horse`.

Here, before the execution of the code, the type of the `animal` variable will be `Animal`. And during the actual execution, the type will be `Horse`.

### 5.1 Invoking Methods

The method invocations depend on the type of the object during the execution of code. So, we can invoke all the methods of the superclass, but when the subclass consists of the same method, it overrides the superclass method and the method of the subclass is invoked.

_Example 1:_ Invoking the overriding method of the subclass

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Animal is sleeping");
    }

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    void eat() {  // overrides the eat() method of Animal class
        System.out.println("Horse is eating");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Horse();

        animal.eat();
        animal.sleep();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Horse is eating
Animal is sleeping
```

In the above code, we are able to invoke the `sleep()` method of superclass `Animal`. And we can see that the `eat()` method of the `Horse` class is invoked instead of the `Animal` class.

_Example 2:_ Invoking a method specific to subclass

#### Code

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Horse extends Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Horse is eating");
    }

    void run() {
        System.out.println("Horse is running");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Horse();

        animal.run();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
file.java:21: error: cannot find symbol
        animal.run();
              ^
```

In the above code, we tried to invoke the `run()` method which is specific to the `Horse` subclass. As we cannot access the methods that are specific to the subclass the error has been thrown.

### 5.2 Accessing Attributes

Accessing of attributes depends on the type of the object before the execution of code.

Before the execution, the type of the variable will be of the superclass, so the attributes will not be inherited. Hence, we cannot access the attributes of the subclass.

_Example 1:_ Accessing superclass attributes

#### Code

```java
class Animal {

    String name = "Alex";

}

class Horse extends Animal {}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Horse();

        System.out.println(animal.name);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

Alex
```

In the above code, we are able to access the attribute of superclass `Animal`

_Example 2:_ Accessing subclass attributes

#### Code

```java
class Animal {}

class Horse extends Animal {

    String breed = "Shire";

}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Horse();

        System.out.println(animal.breed);
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
file.java:13: error: cannot find symbol
        System.out.println(animal.breed);
                                 ^
```

In the above code, as we cannot access the subclass attributes an error has been thrown.

</Section>

<Section>

### Summary

- Method overriding allows a subclass to provide a specific implementation of a method that its superclass already provides.
- Rules of Method Overriding
  - Constructors cannot be overridden
  - The overriding method should have the same return type and parameters
  - A `final` method cannot be overridden
- The `super` keyword can be used to,
  - access the attributes of the superclass when both superclass and subclass have attributes with the same name.
  - call the overridden methods of the superclass inside the subclass.
  - invoke the parameterized superclass constructors explicitly from the subclass constructor.
- Types of inheritance,
  - **Single Inheritance**: It involves extending a single superclass from a single subclass.
  - **Multilevel Inheritance**: In multilevel inheritance, a subclass extends from a superclass and then the same subclass acts as a superclass for another class.
  - **Hierarchical Inheritance**: In hierarchical inheritance, multiple subclasses extend from a single superclass.
- Upcasting,
  - We can specify a superclass as a type to refer to an object of its subclass.
  - We can access all the members of the superclass but can only access a few members like overriding methods of the subclass.
  </Section>

